            <fr:anchor>1387</fr:anchor>
            <fr:addr type="user">sss-001W</fr:addr>
            <fr:route>sss-001W.xml</fr:route>
            <fr:title text="Finite Unambiguity is Not Equivalent to Unambiguity">Finite Unambiguity is Not Equivalent to Unambiguity</fr:title>
          </fr:frontmatter>
          <fr:mainmatter>
            <fr:p>For a while I believed finite unambiguity to be equivalent to the other definitions of unambiguity.</fr:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:anchor>454</fr:anchor>
                <fr:addr type="user">sss-001V</fr:addr>
                <fr:route>sss-001V.xml</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Finite Unambiguity">Finite Unambiguity</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:p>Define a grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> to be <fr:em>finitely unambiguous</fr:em> if <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \And  A \cong  A]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
                <fr:p>There is likely a better name for this.</fr:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:anchor>455</fr:anchor>
                <fr:addr type="user">sss-001T</fr:addr>
                <fr:route>sss-001T.xml</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Unambiguity as Subterminality">Unambiguity as Subterminality</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:p>A grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <fr:em>unambiguous</fr:em> if the unique map into the <fr:link href="sss-001O.xml" title="Terminal Object" addr="sss-001O" type="local">terminal object</fr:link> is a monomorphism. That is, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is a subobject of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\top ]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:anchor>456</fr:anchor>
                <fr:addr type="user">sss-001U</fr:addr>
                <fr:route>sss-001U.xml</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Unambiguity as Unique Map into Codomain">Unambiguity as Unique Map into Codomain</fr:title>
                <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:p>A grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <fr:em>unambiguous</fr:em> if for all grammars <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[B]]></fr:tex> and maps <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e , e' : A \vdash  B]]></fr:tex> we have <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[e = e']]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
                <fr:p>In a category with terminal objects, this is equivalent to unambiguity defined via subterminality.</fr:p>
                <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
                  <fr:frontmatter>
                    <fr:authors>
                      <fr:author>
                        <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                      </fr:author>
                    </fr:authors>
                    <fr:date>
                      <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                      <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                      <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                    </fr:date>
                    <fr:anchor>455</fr:anchor>
                    <fr:addr type="user">sss-001T</fr:addr>
                    <fr:route>sss-001T.xml</fr:route>
                    <fr:title text="Unambiguity as Subterminality">Unambiguity as Subterminality</fr:title>
                    <fr:taxon>Definition</fr:taxon>
                  </fr:frontmatter>
                  <fr:mainmatter>
                    <fr:p>A grammar <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is <fr:em>unambiguous</fr:em> if the unique map into the <fr:link href="sss-001O.xml" title="Terminal Object" addr="sss-001O" type="local">terminal object</fr:link> is a monomorphism. That is, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is a subobject of <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\top ]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
                  </fr:mainmatter>
                </fr:tree>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:p>We may use an analogy from the category of sets, however I had missed the infinite case when translating this idea to grammars.</fr:p>
            <fr:tree show-metadata="false">
              <fr:frontmatter>
                <fr:authors>
                  <fr:author>
                    <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
                  </fr:author>
                </fr:authors>
                <fr:date>
                  <fr:year>2025</fr:year>
                  <fr:month>2</fr:month>
                  <fr:day>12</fr:day>
                </fr:date>
                <fr:anchor>446</fr:anchor>
                <fr:addr type="user">sss-001X</fr:addr>
                <fr:route>sss-001X.xml</fr:route>
                <fr:title text="Sets That Are Isomorphic to Their Own Product">Sets That Are Isomorphic to Their Own Product</fr:title>
              </fr:frontmatter>
              <fr:mainmatter>
                <fr:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> be a set such that <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X \cong  X \times  X]]></fr:tex>. Then either <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[X]]></fr:tex> is the empty set, the singleton set, or an infinite set.</fr:p>
                <fr:p>We may read this intuitively as stating the fixed points of the squaring operation on cardinals has solutions <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[0]]></fr:tex>, <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[1]]></fr:tex>, and all infinite cardinals <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\kappa ]]></fr:tex>.</fr:p>
              </fr:mainmatter>
            </fr:tree>
            <fr:p>It is true that if a grammar is unambiguous, then it is finitely unambiguous. However, the converse does not hold unless the grammar has finitely many parse trees for each string. This finiteness condition is a semantic one. If this proof of unambiguity were to be internalized, then it could maybe be captured through the lens of some <fr:link href="sss-001Y.xml" title="A Grammar of Finite Cardinals" addr="sss-001Y" type="local">grammar of finite cardinals</fr:link>.</fr:p>
            <fr:p>Let <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \colonequals  \textstyle \mathchoice {\textstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\textstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\scriptstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}{\scriptscriptstyle \oplus _{{\mathopen {}\left (n : \mathbb {N}\right )\mathclose {}}}}. \top ]]></fr:tex>. <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> is finitely unambiguous but not unambiguous. The isomorphism bewteen <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[A \And  A]]></fr:tex> amounts to building a bijection between <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {N}]]></fr:tex> and <fr:tex display="inline"><![CDATA[\mathbb {N} \times  \mathbb {N}]]></fr:tex>, which is straightforward.</fr:p>
          </fr:mainmatter>
        </fr:tree>
        <fr:tree show-metadata="true" expanded="false" toc="false" numbered="false">
          <fr:frontmatter>
            <fr:authors>
              <fr:author>
                <fr:link href="stevenschaefer.xml" title="Steven Schaefer" addr="stevenschaefer" type="local">Steven Schaefer</fr:link>
              </fr:author>
